nsncd (1.6.0) unstable; urgency=low

  nsncd now runs as the unprivileged system user and group `_nsncd`
  which the package creates on install. The service unit also applies
  systemd sandboxing; it drops all capabilities and enables
  ProtectSystem=strict, PrivateTmp and RestrictAddressFamilies.

  Most NSS modules work unchanged, including sss, ldap (nslcd), winbind,
  systemd, files, extrausers and cache, because they read world-readable
  files or talk to a privileged daemon over a socket.

  Some legacy modules need root to work correctly. One example is PADL's
  libnss_ldap configured with rootbinddn, which reads /etc/ldap.secret
  only when running as root. Without root, it may fall back to an
  anonymous bind and return incomplete results. If you use such a module,
  restore the previous behaviour with a drop-in (`systemctl edit nsncd`):

    [Service]
    User=root
    Group=root
    CapabilityBoundingSet=~
    PrivateTmp=false

  Then run `systemctl restart nsncd`.

 -- James Morris <jamesm@twosigma.com>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 10:17:26 -0500
